"GitHub - Oraclizer/statesync-gkr: Rust prover for sparse-Merkle state transitions, with GKR and sumcheck mechanized in Isabelle/HOL · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":300}); Total lines: 523\nL270: These mechanisms draw on measurements, source structure, rejection controls and formal models, rather than nine independently isolated causal experiments. The compiler-to-protocol model is mechanized. Selected Rust contract proofs and a conditional forward acceptance theorem provide a review map for the actual verifier checks; the Rust/model value relation remains a premise. 【116†Refinement evidence】 describes the reusable checks and remaining obligations.\nL271: Licensing is described under 【125†License and citation】.\nL272: The 【121†implementation architecture】 connects these mechanisms to their source and application conditions. 【115†Performance and operating profiles】 gives exact values, statistical units and the separate memory, computation and serving measurements. The 【126†controlled capacity replay】 provides the small Linux caller/memory check and the separate dataset layout.\nL273: ## What you can use\nL274:   * SMT operation: use `StateSyncProver` with `SyncRequest` for membership, absence or a single-leaf transition (【127†guide†oraclizer.io】).\nL275:   * Prepared requests: call `prepare` once for a kind and configuration, then `prove_sync_op_prepared` and `verify_sync_op_prepared` per request (【128†guide†oraclizer.io】).\nL276:   * CPU jobs: construct each witness with `make_job_prepared`, then run independent jobs through `prove_batch_parallel` (【129†guide†oraclizer.io】).\nL277:   * Encoded proof: use `encode_sync_result` and `verify_encoded_sync_op` (【130†guide†oraclizer.io】).\nL278:   * Proof service: use the serving components in 【131†`benches/serving`】 for bounded admission, framed replies bound to job identifiers, a return budget and continuous workers, or run their reference proof server.\nL279:   * Another circuit frontend: use the `sumcheck` and `gkr` modules and supply the frontend's statement and input binding (【132†guide†oraclizer.io】).\nL280:   * Model and implementation boundary: read 【100†Formal verification】 and the 【133†formal study†arxiv.org】.\nL281: For direct SMT checks and the GKR boundary, see 【134†Choosing a path】.\nL282: ## Quick start\nL283: \nL284: Clone the repository for this local example. Research, development and other non-production use are permitted by 【102†LICENSE】. Pin a specific current commit when recording a replay.\nL285: \nL286:     git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr.git\nL287:     cd statesync-gkr\nL288:     rustup show\nL289:     cargo run --release --locked --example state_sync_prove_verify\nL290: \nL291: Expected output:\nL292: \nL293:     `honest-proof=PASS\nL294:     tampered-proof=REJECTED\nL295:     secondary-finalized=false\nL296:     `\nL297: The example builds one synthetic membership request, proves it, verifies the honest result and rejects a modified proof. It uses the pinned Rust toolchain and the release lock file. The packages are distributed as source; pin a Git tag or commit when integrating them into another application. The online 【135†Quickstart†oraclizer.io】 reads through the example, and 【136†Installation†oraclizer.io】 shows how to add the packages as a dependency.\nL298: For proving speed, build with `RUSTFLAGS=\"-Ctarget-cpu=native\"` on the machine that runs the prover. In the v1.2.0 one-thread measurements the default release build took 1.66–1.71 times as long per prepared proof; both builds produce the same proof bytes. 【137†Build for proving speed】 gives the measurements.\nL299: Requirement  | Version\nL300: --- | ---\nL301: Rust  | 1.96.1, pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; declared minimum 1.85 (`rust-version`)\nL302: Hosted CI  | Linux x86_64\nL303: Isabelle sessions  | Isabelle2025-2 with the AFP release `afp-2026-08-05`\nL304: ## Minimal Rust integration\nL305: The facade consumes an operation, its private Merkle witness and the public inputs that the caller expects. A checked call handles both construction errors and a rejected verifier result:\nL306: \nL307:     use statesync_gkr::{StateSyncProver, SyncRequest, SyncResult};\nL308: \nL309:     pub fn prove_checked(\nL310:         prover: &StateSyncProver,\nL311:         request: &SyncRequest,\nL312:     ) -> Result<SyncResult, String> {\nL313:         let result = prover\nL314:             .prove_sync_op(request)\nL315:             .map_err(|error| format!(\"proving failed: {error:?}\"))?;\nL316:         if !prover.verify_sync_op(request, &result) {\nL317:             return Err(\"the proof or its request binding was rejected\".to_owned());\nL318:         }\nL319:         Ok(result)\nL320:     }\nL321: 【114†Usage and API boundary】 contains a complete application, dependency pin, supported input format, prepared execution and error handling. The 【138†API reference†oraclizer.io】 lists every public module. The caller obtains trusted roots and witnesses from its own state store; the library does not authenticate an external database or establish consensus. 【139†Trust boundaries†oraclizer.io】 separates what a successful verification establishes from the application's responsibilities.\nL322: The current verifier reconstructs the circuit input from that witness, including the leaf hash and the complete old/new Merkle paths.\nL323: ## CPU measurements\nL324: \nL325: Self-measured on two AMD EPYC 9R45 sockets: 192 physical CPU cores, SMT off, 384 GiB installed RAM, Linux, Rust 1.96.1 with `-Ctarget-cpu=native`. Depth 24, batch 768, 192 workers, two-field occupied payloads. The v1.2.0 rows come from the same instance type as the v1.1.0 rows, running Amazon Linux 2023 with kernel 6.18 instead of the earlier kernel 6.8; a same-day v1.1.0 membership rerun came within 0.4% of its earlier rate.\nL326: All columns are inner proofs/s. The witness columns include proving; the last column parallelizes witness creation in the benchmark caller.\nL327: Operation  | Release  | Proving only  | Serial witness  | Parallel witness\nL328: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL329: Membership  | v1.2.0  | 7,713.71  | 1,323.71  | 6,323.95\nL330:  | v1.1.0  | 3,207.74  | 1,060.35  | 2,881.55\nL331: Non-membership  | v1.2.0  | 7,220.36  | 1,313.25  | 5,899.04\nL332:  | v1.1.0  | 2,930.85  | 1,021.87  | 2,654.03\nL333: Update  | v1.2.0  | 5,390.07  | 716.64  | 4,782.21\nL334:  | v1.1.0  | 2,092.03  | 594.56  | 1,984.00\nL335: Against v1.1.0 measured on the same host the same day, v1.2.0 proving-only rates are 2.41–2.56 times higher at 192 workers and 2.53–2.71 times higher on one worker. Each value is the median of three process-level rates; each rate uses the median of three measured batch intervals. The parallel-witness column is benchmark-caller orchestration around the existing public APIs. Timers exclude encoding, hash/control checks and delivery.\nL336: Fixtures have independent roots; these are inner-job rates, not sustained service throughput or sequential state commits. The 【140†controlled CPU note】 provides run ranges, latency, RSS, direct-SMT predicate scope, raw samples and replay.\nL337: A separate five-process supplement measures the broader computation interval: parallel witness creation → proving → encoding → encoded-proof acceptance. At the same depth 24, batch 768 and 192 workers:\nL338: \nL339: Both columns are inner proofs/s for the complete prepared local computation.\nL340: Operation  | Release  | Parallel output  | Serial output\nL341: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL342: Membership  | v1.2.0  | 2,264.06  | 153.81\nL343:  | v1.1.0  | 1,593.17  | 148.98\nL344: Nonmembership  | v1.2.0  | 2,107.57  | 149.30\nL345:  | v1.1.0  | 1,431.66  | 144.01\nL346: Update  | v1.2.0  | 1,815.31  | 125.59\nL347:  | v1.1.0  | 1,167.49  | 121.01\nL348: Both columns create witnesses and prove in parallel; only encoding and encoded verification change mode. Paired serial/parallel timings of that full interval yield 14.10–14.64 times lower local-batch time for v1.2.0 and 9.71–10.71 times for v1.1.0 across the three operations. This range summarizes paired ratios, not ratios of the throughput medians above. Preparation, transport, post-timer hashing/logging and state commits are outside this interval. These are computation rates, not service SPS.\nL349: See the 【141†supplemental study】 for five-process ranges, direct preparation costs, paired wiring, input types and raw data.\nL350: ## Measured serving and memory profiles\nL351: \nL352: The serving experiments send complete encoded proofs over private TCP to a separate generator/verifier frontend. Their endpoint is cryptographic acceptance of the original request, including queueing and transport. The v1.2.0 runs use the reference proof server from `benches/serving` in continuous mode, with v1.1.0 run on the same day and VM types as the reference:\nL353:   * 192 cores / 384 GiB, one connection: every timed request was accepted within one second at 3,300 requests/s in three 60-second runs (p99 35.4–38.5 ms) and in a 30-minute run of 5,940,000 requests (p99 35.3 ms). v1.1.0 met one second at 1,000 requests/s in the earlier campaign and at 1,150 requests/s, the highest rate tried for it, on the same day.\nL354:   * Two connections to the same 192-core server: 15-second screens met one second at every rate from 3,400 to 5,000 requests/s. At 5,000/s the 48-core frontend reached its own limit, so 5,000/s is a lower bound for the server.\nL355:   * 192 cores, 1,000 requests/s: p99 33.7–33.9 ms in three 60-second runs, against 433.8–475.1 ms for v1.1.0 on the same day.\nL356:   * 48 and 96 cores: in screens with one 15-second run per rate, the highest offered rate with every timed request accepted within one second was 2,000 and 3,000 requests/s; these were not confirmed by longer runs. In same-day screens of 15 and 20 seconds, v1.1.0 passed 500/s and failed at its next measured rate of 1,000/s.\nL357:   * Above capacity: offered 5,100 requests/s under a 2,760-MiB memory limit, the 192-core server's sampled peak RSS was 548,064 KiB (535.2 MiB, about 19% of the limit; 402,948 KiB or 393.5 MiB by GNU time) and it exited normally; its return budget filled while the excess requests waited at the frontend.\nL358:   * Compute cost: about USD 0.87 per million requests at 3,300/s on the 192-core VM. For v1.1.0 it was USD 2.87 at 1,000/s in the earlier campaign and at most USD 2.50 at 1,150/s on the same day, the highest rate tried for it. The estimate divides the VM's on-demand hourly price by the measured rate for continuous operation and leaves out the frontend, storage and network.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2104.07857] ZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.07857)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.07857\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 176\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 16 Apr 2021]\nL9: # Title:ZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Samyam Rajbhandari】, 【6†Olatunji Ruwase】, 【7†Jeff Rasley】, 【8†Shaden Smith】, 【9†Yuxiong He】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled ZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning, by Samyam Rajbhandari and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【10†View PDF】 【11†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:In the last three years, the largest dense deep learning models have grown over 1000x to reach hundreds of billions of parameters, while the GPU memory has only grown by 5x (16 GB to 80 GB). Therefore, the growth in model scale has been supported primarily though system innovations that allow large models to fit in the aggregate GPU memory of multiple GPUs. However, we are getting close to the GPU memory wall.\nL17: It requires 800 NVIDIA V100 GPUs just to fit a trillion parameter model for training, and such clusters are simply out of reach for most data scientists. In addition, training models at that scale requires complex combinations of parallelism techniques that puts a big burden on the data scientists to refactor their model.\nL18: > In this paper we present ZeRO-Infinity, a novel heterogeneous system technology that leverages GPU, CPU, and NVMe memory to allow for unprecedented model scale on limited resources without requiring model code refactoring. At the same time it achieves excellent training throughput and scalability, unencumbered by the limited CPU or NVMe bandwidth. ZeRO-Infinity can fit models with tens and even hundreds of trillions of parameters for training on current generation GPU clusters.\nL19: It can be used to fine-tune trillion parameter models on a single NVIDIA DGX-2 node, making large models more accessible. In terms of training throughput and scalability, it sustains over 25 petaflops on 512 NVIDIA V100 GPUs(40% of peak), while also demonstrating super linear scalability. An open source implementation of ZeRO-Infinity is available through DeepSpeed, a deep learning optimization library that makes distributed training easy, efficient, and effective.\nL20: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Performance (cs.PF)\nL21: Cite as:  | 【12†arXiv:2104.07857】 [cs.DC]\nL22:    | (or 【13†arXiv:2104.07857v1】 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL23:    | 【14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2104.07857†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL